<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Grade:</b> 4th Grade (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Scenario:</b> Shopping at the Market</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Theme:</b> I Need Five Yucas.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: text: Explain the main ideas of class readings to absent students. concept: Use examples to explain math concepts to peers.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can explain the main ideas from a class reading about the market with basic words and gestures (e.g., points to apples and says, 'We buy apples at the market.' 'They are cheap.'). Can use objects to explain quantities and prices (e.g., holds two apples and says, 'These are two apples.' or 'The apples cost two dollars.'). Can use simple counting with classmates to show math concepts (e.g., holds up three fingers and says, 'three apples').</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%; background-color: #f2f2f2;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%; background-color: #f2f2f2;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%; background-color: #f2f2f2;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows flashcards of market items (pineapple, cassava, potatoes, money). Students chorally name the items. The teacher asks, "What do we buy at the market?" "Do you like pineapples? How many do you want?" Students respond with simple phrases (e.g., "Yes, I like pineapple.", "I want two pineapples."). The teacher reviews numbers 1-10 through counting objects.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up two apples (or pictures of apples) and says, "These are two apples. They cost two dollars." (using /m/ sound for 'money'). Then holds up a picture of a pineapple and says, "This is a pineapple. It costs five dollars. It is expensive." The teacher points to numbers on a price chart. The teacher then models explaining a simple price comparison: "In this market, the apple costs one dollar. In that market, it costs two dollars. This market is cheaper." using gestures and clear pronunciation of /p/ in 'pineapple'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Am I showing you how to buy or how to explain prices?" (Expected answer: "Explain prices.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do I use words AND my hands to explain?" (Expected answer: "Yes, words and hands.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Shopping at the Market")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A simple "Market Price List" chart showing items (e.g., pineapple, cassava, potatoes), quantities, and prices from two different imaginary markets (e.g., "Sunny Market" and "Green Market"). The chart includes numbers up to 20 for prices. A short reading excerpt about buying items from Lesson 2 will be briefly displayed.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Can explain the main ideas from a class reading about the market with basic words and gestures. Can use objects to explain quantities and prices.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher points to an item on the "Market Price List" (e.g., pineapple) and asks, "How much is the pineapple at Sunny Market?" Students find the price (e.g., "Two dollars.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher asks, "Is the cassava cheap at Green Market? Yes or No?" (Students respond based on the list).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher holds up a picture of five potatoes and asks, "How many potatoes do I need?" (Students say: "Five potatoes.").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Mediation)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> WH-Questions ('How much is it?'), Quantities ('I need five potatoes.'), Present Simple ('I want three apples and one banana.'). Vocabulary: market, price, item, store, cost, pineapple, cassava, potatoes, buy, sell, need, want, cheap, expensive, numbers 1-100. Pronunciation of /p/ in 'pineapple' and /m/ in 'money'. Pragmatic: Asking about prices, Describing quantities, Discourse marker ('and'). Sociolinguistic: Polite expressions ('Excuse me').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. <b>Controlled Practice (Sentence Completion):</b> Students receive a worksheet with sentences about the "Market Price List" from Stage 2. They complete the sentences to explain prices and quantities. E.g., "In Sunny Market, the pineapple costs _____ dollars." "I want _____ potatoes. They are _____." The teacher monitors pronunciation of numbers and target sounds.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. <b>Semi-controlled Practice (Item Explanation):</b> In pairs, students use realia or flashcards of market items with written prices (e.g., 'pineapple $5', 'cassava $3'). Student A picks an item and asks, "Excuse me, how much is the [item]?" Student B explains, "The [item] costs [number] dollars. It is [cheap/expensive]." They swap roles, focusing on clear articulation and using simple gestures to indicate quantity. The teacher reminds them to use the /p/ and /m/ sounds correctly.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. <b>Pair Interaction (Shopping List Explanation):</b> Students are given simple shopping lists (e.g., "Shopping List: 2 apples, 4 bananas"). In pairs, Student A explains their shopping list to Student B, mediating the information: "I need two apples and four bananas." Student B listens and verifies by pointing to pictures of the items or by simple repetition. They then explain prices based on a small provided price chart (e.g., "The apples cost two dollars.").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Shopping at the Market")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Task:</b> Students will present and *explain* their "Price Comparison Chart" (Project 2 from the 21st Century Skills Focus) in small groups. They will mediate information about prices of items from two different markets.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> In groups of 3-4, students use their prepared "Price Comparison Chart" (which could be a simple drawing or a table with pictures). Each student takes a turn to *explain* the price of at least two items (e.g., pineapple, cassava, potatoes) from their chart, comparing them between two markets. They will explain quantities as well. For example, "In Sunny Market, one pineapple costs two dollars. In Green Market, one pineapple costs three dollars. Sunny Market is cheaper." They must use simple words and gestures to help their explanation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Actions:</b> The teacher circulates, listens to explanations, and encourages the use of target vocabulary (market, price, item, cheap, expensive), grammar (WH-questions, quantities, present simple), and politeness markers ('Excuse me, how much is it?'). The teacher notes effective mediation strategies (gestures, clear words).</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students work in pairs. Student A presents a simplified "Price Comparison Chart" (or points to one on the board) and explains the price of one item from two different markets, comparing them (e.g., "The pineapple in this market is five dollars. In that market, it is four dollars. This market is cheaper."). Student B listens and asks one "How much...?" question. They then switch roles. Students are encouraged to use gestures to aid their explanation and to count fingers to show quantities (e.g., "three apples").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Observation Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of /p/ (pineapple, potatoes) and /m/ (money, market) and numbers.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target vocabulary (market, price, shopping list, item, cost, pineapple, cassava, potatoes, cheap, expensive, numbers 1-100).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of explanation: Can explain main ideas about prices and quantities using basic words.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of gestures/objects to support explanation (e.g., pointing, holding up fingers).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can answer "How much...?" questions.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick exit ticket:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can explain the price of ______ (item) to my friend." (Yes / A little / No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can use my hands to help explain numbers." (Yes / A little / No)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy for me when I explained the prices of items?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What can I do better next time to explain more clearly?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well during the mediation tasks? Were students able to explain prices and quantities effectively using simple language and gestures?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for students who struggled with explaining or using target vocabulary/grammar? (e.g., more sentence frames for comparison, specific drills for number pronunciation).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson completes the "Shopping at the Market" theme. The next lesson will begin a new theme, perhaps "At the Zoo," building on vocabulary and simple sentence structures for description and quantity, encouraging students to apply their newfound mediation skills in a new context.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>